WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.040
Who almost played Haymitch&nbsp;
instead of Woody Harrelson?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:07.680
Which famous musician tried and failed to&nbsp;
get his songs played in "Catching Fire?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.880
Keep watching to learn the untold&nbsp;
truth about the "Hunger Games" movies.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:15.760
The "Hunger Games" series ended up&nbsp;
grossing just under $3 billion worldwide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:21.040
making it one of the biggest film franchises of&nbsp;
the 2010s. It was an impressive feat, especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:26.000
since the source material was still fresh in the&nbsp;
public consciousness. It wasn't a saga getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.600
by on decades of nostalgia, but rather one that&nbsp;
resonated powerfully at the moment. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:37.280
the franchise had no trouble securing sizable&nbsp;
box office returns every time it hit theaters.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.720
Given the series' enormous financial victories, it&nbsp;
might be surprising to learn how little Lionsgate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:48.800
paid for the film rights. Back in 2008, Newsweek&nbsp;
reported that the studio shelled out just $200,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.840
to bring Katniss Everdeen and&nbsp;
her comrades to the big screen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.720
While far from chump change, that seems&nbsp;
like an impossibly low sum now given the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:00.640
franchise's success. The return on&nbsp;
investment wound up being massive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:04.640
with the eventual box office grosses far&nbsp;
exceeding the price paid for the rights.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:09.840
Nowadays, it's nearly impossible to imagine&nbsp;
anyone but Jennifer Lawrence playing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:14.160
iconic lead role of Katniss Everdeen.&nbsp;
The first "Hunger Games" film came out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:18.720
at a pivotal time in Lawrence's career: her&nbsp;
star was rising after her acclaimed turn in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:24.160
2010's "Winter's Bone" and her debut as&nbsp;
Mystique in 2011's "X-Men: First Class."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:28.000
Plus, "The Hunger Games" premiered just&nbsp;
months ahead of "Silver Linings Playbook."

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.400
It might seem like Lawrence was fated to play&nbsp;
Katniss, but her selection was far from a sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.840
thing at the time. According to EW, the casting&nbsp;
search for the role spanned far and wide, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:42.400
at least 30 different women being considered for&nbsp;
the part. Lawrence was a top contender from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:47.360
beginning, but young stars like Hailee Steinfeld,&nbsp;
Saoirse Ronan, and future "Divergent" star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:51.920
Shailene Woodley were also in the running.&nbsp;
The process considered performers ranging from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:58.080
ages 14 to 24, and since there was initially no&nbsp;
frontrunner, it was open season for young actors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:01.920
Eventually, Lawrence — hot off her Oscar&nbsp;
nod for "Winter's Bone" — would score&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:06.080
the role of Katniss, changing her career&nbsp;
and the "Hunger Games" franchise forever.

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.480
“Thank you for your consideration.”

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:12.880
While not quite as extensive as&nbsp;
the hunt for Katniss Everdeen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:18.160
Lionsgate's search for a director to helm the&nbsp;
first "Hunger Games" was still quite an affair.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:23.200
In September 2010, Deadline reported that the&nbsp;
primary frontrunners for the job were Gary Ross,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:28.480
Sam Mendes, and David Slade. Francis Lawrence,&nbsp;
who would go on to direct all of the "Hunger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:32.800
Games" sequels, was also considered for the&nbsp;
first film, but he was ultimately passed over.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:37.120
It's easy to see why Lionsgate gravitated&nbsp;
toward each of these filmmakers for "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:42.240
Hunger Games," even though they each had vastly&nbsp;
different qualifications. Slade, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:46.640
was hot off of helming the third "Twilight" movie,&nbsp;
"Eclipse," which had reached a box office high for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:50.960
the franchise. Meanwhile, Mendes didn't have&nbsp;
experience directing movies aimed at teens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:55.120
but his reputation as director of the Best&nbsp;
Picture-winning film "American Beauty" would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:59.680
have lent instant prestige to "The Hunger&nbsp;
Games." Eventually, though, Lionsgate settled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:04.800
on Gary Ross — another director familiar with&nbsp;
awards season thanks to his 2003 sports drama&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:09.280
"Seabiscuit." With Ross at the helm, a key&nbsp;
part of the "Hunger Games" equation was solved.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:14.240
The casting for Katniss, Peeta, and Gale&nbsp;
took up much of the media's attention when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:18.000
it came to getting actors assembled for "The&nbsp;
Hunger Games," but they were far from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.320
only characters in need of talented stars.&nbsp;
One of the most important figures in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.640
whole franchise is Haymitch Abernathy,&nbsp;
a former Hunger Games victor who's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:30.400
shell of his former self by the time&nbsp;
he's assigned as a mentor to Katniss.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:34.560
The part required someone who could be funny&nbsp;
while also providing a believable sense of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:39.200
sorrow and trauma. It's a complex part, but&nbsp;
also one of the most important to the story..

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:43.920
In April 2011, as "The Hunger Games" was&nbsp;
nearing the start of principal photography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:48.000
Vulture reported that John C. Reilly would&nbsp;
be offered the role. A character actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.920
known for working in all genres,&nbsp;
Reilly seemed like a great fit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:55.920
He could definitely handle the comic side of&nbsp;
Haymitch, and his dramatic performances in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:00.480
films like "Chicago" demonstrated his ability&nbsp;
to depict vulnerability as well. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:05.440
despite how promising the casting sounded, it&nbsp;
wasn't to be. Woody Harrelson was ultimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:09.760
selected for the part instead, and reprised&nbsp;
the role in all three "Hunger Games" sequels.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.840
“Oh, joy, why don’t you join us?”

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:18.880
Once "The Hunger Games" became a box-office&nbsp;
smash, it was immediately apparent that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:23.360
other two books in the series, "Catching Fire"&nbsp;
and "Mockingjay," would be adapted into feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:27.760
films as well. Even before the first movie's&nbsp;
release, Lionsgate showed its confidence in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:33.520
franchise by scheduling a "Catching Fire"&nbsp;
adaptation for release in November 2013.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:37.200
It was a bold move that showed high&nbsp;
expectations for "The Hunger Games," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:40.480
it also allowed for "Catching Fire" to&nbsp;
come out just a year and a half later.

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:45.040
“Now, because of her, they all pose a threat. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:48.640
because of her, they all&nbsp;
think they’re invincible.”

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:53.920
Just a few days into the theatrical run of "The&nbsp;
Hunger Games," director Gary Ross announced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:58.160
that he would not be directing "Catching&nbsp;
Fire." According to EW, Ross admitted that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:02.480
the sequel's rapid production schedule didn't&nbsp;
give him adequate time to prepare the project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:07.120
making him uneager to return. When Lionsgate&nbsp;
quickly got to work finding a replacement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:12.080
director for "Catching Fire," the competition was&nbsp;
quickly narrowed down to two names: Bennett Miller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:17.440
and Francis Lawrence. At the time, Miller seemed&nbsp;
like a solid pick, as he'd recently helmed awards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:22.480
season favorites like "Capote" and "Moneyball."&nbsp;
Francis Lawrence — no relation to Jennifer — was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:26.960
a genre movie veteran known for films like&nbsp;
"Constantine" and "I Am Legend." Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:31.440
the odds tilted in favor of Lawrence, who&nbsp;
ultimately won the gig and went on to direct both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:37.200
parts of the "Mockingjay" adaptation as well.
 
Screenwriter Michael Arndt hit the ground running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:40.960
at the start of his career in Hollywood.&nbsp;
His first produced script was "Little Miss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:45.520
Sunshine," which would go on to win him an&nbsp;
Academy Award. Right after that, Arndt penned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:50.080
the screenplay for "Toy Story 3," making him&nbsp;
two-for-two in critically acclaimed scripts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:54.240
One of his many gigs after working on the&nbsp;
Pixar film was rewriting the screenplay for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:59.120
"The Hunger Games: Catching Fire" — an experience&nbsp;
that wound up being a major challenge to pull off.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:03.760
In an interview with The Script Lab, Arndt&nbsp;
revealed that the primary issue he faced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:08.640
with rewriting "Catching Fire" was time. Every&nbsp;
screenwriter wishes they had more time to write&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:13.760
and edit their scripts, but Arndt had the specific&nbsp;
challenge of finalizing it just 12 weeks before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:18.720
filming began. With such limited time on his&nbsp;
hands, Arndt needed all the help he could get.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:23.120
Fortunately, co-writer Simon Beaufoy and the rest&nbsp;
of the "Hunger Games" creative team were there to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:27.840
provide exactly that. Through extensive&nbsp;
collaboration, Arndt made sure that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:32.320
screenplay for "Catching Fire" was ready to go&nbsp;
just in time for the cameras to start rolling.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:36.880
Ed Sheeran is such a popular artist that&nbsp;
it's hard to imagine anyone turning down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:41.040
the opportunity to work with him. He's had&nbsp;
big success contributing songs to feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:45.120
films like "The Hobbit: The Desolation&nbsp;
of Smaug" and "Yesterday." However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:48.400
Lionsgate actually passed on the chance&nbsp;
to work with Sheeran on "The Hunger Games:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:52.480
Catching Fire," rejecting a trio of tunes&nbsp;
that he proposed for the film's soundtrack.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:57.840
In an interview with radio station Click 98.9,&nbsp;
Sheeran said that he pitched three original songs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:02.640
for the "Catching Fire" soundtrack, all of which&nbsp;
were turned down. Sheeran also noted that he had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:08.000
similar experience having song proposals rejected&nbsp;
for the "Twilight" movies. Despite the setbacks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:12.000
he said that he still wanted to produce music&nbsp;
for feature films as a way of expanding his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:17.200
creative horizons. Of course, given the enormous&nbsp;
success that Sheeran's had in the years since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:21.840
"Catching Fire," missing out on the film obviously&nbsp;
wasn't the end of the world for the performer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:26.080
Still, having so many original songs rejected&nbsp;
would be a disappointment for anyone, even him.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:31.520
It's easy to see why the third "Hunger Games"&nbsp;
book, "Mockingjay," was split into two separate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:36.080
movies. Both the "Harry Potter" and "Twilight"&nbsp;
series adaptations had their final installments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:40.240
split into two parts, and "The Hobbit"&nbsp;
spawned an entire trilogy all on its own.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:44.080
It makes sense why "The Hunger Games" would&nbsp;
follow suit: after all, turning one book into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:49.360
multiple movies means extra opportunities for the&nbsp;
studio to milk a big-name IP at the box office.

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:52.320
“If we burn, you burn with us!”

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:57.120
However, the official reason given by director&nbsp;
Francis Lawrence for splitting the "Mockingjay"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:01.600
book into multiple films was to emphasize two&nbsp;
distinct tones in Katniss Everdeen's final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:06.160
adventure. Lawrence saw the first "Mockingjay"&nbsp;
movie as being more "internal" — a quiet but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:10.480
ominous political affair — whereas the&nbsp;
second film was a larger-scale war story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:14.560
For Lawrence, trying to cram these different&nbsp;
aesthetics into one film wouldn't have worked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:19.600
so he welcomed the opportunity to split the story&nbsp;
into two feature films. Of course, the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:24.320
Lionsgate got an extra blockbuster out of this&nbsp;
decision was a nice bonus for the studio as well.

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:30.240
In February 2014, Hollywood lost an acting&nbsp;
legend when Philip Seymour Hoffman passed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:35.440
away from a drug overdose at the age of 46. The&nbsp;
actor left behind a staggering body of work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:39.120
having earned three Oscar nominations&nbsp;
and a win during his lifetime.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:42.960
Hoffman's death came toward the end of filming&nbsp;
on the two "Mockingjay" titles, in which he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:48.240
appeared as gamemaker and rebel commander Plutarch&nbsp;
Heavensbee. Since Heavensbee was such an important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:53.440
part of the books and the "Catching Fire" movie,&nbsp;
Hoffman's death had a major impact on production.

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:58.400
In the end, only minor changes had to be made&nbsp;
in the wake of Hoffman's passing. He had just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:02.720
one major scene left to film for the second part&nbsp;
of "Mockingjay," and all of his material for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:07.040
first part had been completed. The big remaining&nbsp;
scene was supposed to show Heavensbee meeting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:12.720
Katniss after her third-act imprisonment. Instead,&nbsp;
the scene was changed so that Haymitch visited her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:17.760
and read a note that Heavensbee had written. It&nbsp;
was a bittersweet moment for fans of the beloved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:22.720
actor — a clear reminder of Hoffman's absence and&nbsp;
the tremendous loss that Hollywood had suffered.

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:27.200
For a brief moment, the cast of "The&nbsp;
Hunger Games: Mockingjay – Part 2"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:31.440
included Lily Rabe in the role of Commander&nbsp;
Lyme. Being a part of the blockbuster's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:35.280
star-studded cast would have been a big&nbsp;
deal for Rabe, but she eventually had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:39.280
drop out of the production, with Gwendoline&nbsp;
Christie replacing her in the final cut.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:44.240
Such drastic casting shifts in productions&nbsp;
as big as "Mockingjay" are sometimes due to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:47.840
behind-the-scenes drama, but according&nbsp;
to Rabe, that wasn't the case for her.

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:53.280
Speaking to Collider in 2021, Rabe explained that&nbsp;
when she initially signed onto "Mockingjay," she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:58.240
didn't have any scheduling conflicts. However, the&nbsp;
shooting dates kept getting bumped up, eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:03.040
causing an interference with Rabe's Shakespeare in&nbsp;
the Park production of "Much Ado About Nothing."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:07.280
Since she had committed to the play first, Rabe&nbsp;
had to drop out of "Mockingjay." According to her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:11.680
the producers of the film were very understanding&nbsp;
and supported Rabe's choice when it became clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:15.680
she'd have to pick one or the other.&nbsp;
Though far from ideal circumstances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:19.360
there are certainly worse ways to leave&nbsp;
the cast of such a high-profile movie.

